WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.400
Let's bring

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.301
in Democratic Congressman

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.335
Adam Schiff of California,

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.837
who was a member of the January six

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.038
committee. Congressman.

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:05.972
What was your reaction

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.040
when you first saw this report

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.141
and recommended charges

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.242
against 39 people?

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:11.077
Did that number seem high?

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:11.578
Do you did

00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:12.212
any of the names

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:14.881
on this list surprise you?

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.384
You know, what stood out to me, Jake,

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:19.552
is the fact that the grand jury

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.288
really did a diligent effort in that

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:23.890
the vote totals,

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:24.157
you know,

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:26.192
for each defendant really varied.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.794
So when they consider Lindsey Graham,

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:28.561
I think the vote was

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.630
something like 13 to seven.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.998
When they looked at one of the

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.833
then existing Georgia senators,

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.035
it was a different vote count.

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:36.002
The other senator

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.404
to get another vote count.

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:38.772
That tells me the grand jurors

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:40.840
took their responsibilities seriously.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.809
They weighed the evidence particular

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:43.910
to each defendant.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.245
It wasn't like you know,

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:46.980
kind of a blanket decision.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.481
That's what they're supposed to do.

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:50.517
And the fact that they recommended

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.253
charging Lindsey Graham and by the way,

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:53.820
I think Lindsey

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.221
Graham's explanation

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.090
doesn't pass the laugh test.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.259
You don't as a senator or House member

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.660
or other elected official,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.062
call a secretary of state

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:03.530
in some other state

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.032
and try to get them to toss out votes.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:08.201
That is not the least bit

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:09.903
part of the job description.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:11.871
And he's lucky not to be indicted.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:13.873
But here the prosecutor made a decision

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:15.675
that their burden of proof

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:16.676
is a different standard

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:17.510
than the grand jury.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.079
The grand jury decides

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:20.346
if there's probable cause.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:21.948
But a prosecutor's got to decide,

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:22.615
can I prove this

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.384
beyond a reasonable doubt?

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:26.019
And, you know, with the varying

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.488
conflicting interpretations of that call,

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:29.823
among other things,

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:30.757
I think the prosecutors

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:32.425
made a rational decision.

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.326
You know, it's interesting.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:34.594
Senator Graham

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.096
criticizing

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:37.831
the fact that he was almost charged,

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:38.865
even though he was not.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:39.466
I mean,

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:41.701
the only witness against him

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:43.770
is a conservative Republican

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.773
who endorsed Donald Trump.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:47.474
And the person

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:49.008
who decided to not prosecute

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:50.677
him is a liberal Democrat

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:51.878
who endorsed Joe Biden.

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:53.713
But be that as it may

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.381
I want to tick through the votes

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.983
to bring charges and go.

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.219
I can mention to think,

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:01.721
you know, Lindsey Graham is an attorney.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.591
I think he was in one of the adjutant

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:09.496
in the JAG Corps in the military.

00:02:09.496 --> 00:02:10.497
This is someone who knows

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:10.730
the law,

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:11.731
both as a senator,

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.533
as a practicing lawyer.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.135
He knows where the line is

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.336
and not to cross the line.

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.303
He shouldn't have been anywhere

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.571
near the line.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:19.272
So, as I said,

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.708
I think he's gotten off lucky here.

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:22.609
And I do think

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:24.177
even though he's not been indicted,

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:25.945
it will be a deterrent.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.380
To other members of Congress

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.582
not to meddle, not to approach the line

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.351
when it comes to suggesting votes

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.218
ought to be tossed out

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.853
to favor your candidate.

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:34.921
I want to tick through the votes

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.823
to bring charges or not bring charges

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:37.590
against some of Trump's

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.525
top allies,

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:39.626
Michael Flynn,

00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:41.427
Boris Epshteyn and Cleta Mitchell.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.363
The grand jury voted the same way

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:44.164
for all three of them.

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:46.466
20 said to bring yet

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.301
to bring votes to bring charges, rather.

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.369
24.

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:51.471
Flynn Epstein and Clayton Mitchell,

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:54.274
one for each one of them said no.

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:56.609
And zero abstained.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:03:00.213
That's nearly unanimous for all three.

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:01.347
Yet charges

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.115
were not brought

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:05.618
against any one of these individuals.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:06.686
What do you make of that?

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:10.590
You know, it could be,

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:10.857
as you

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:11.858
were discussing earlier,

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:12.792
that some of these people

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:14.294
have decided to cooperate

00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:15.461
in the case of the folks

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.496
you just mentioned.

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:17.230
That seems, though,

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.198
very unlikely

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.366
These are some hardcore people.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.802
My guess is that the prosecutors

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:24.571
just made a different judgment

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:25.672
about what were the

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.573
what was the availability

00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:28.308
of the legal defenses

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:30.310
and they took a different view of things

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:31.110
than the grand jurors.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.346
That is not at all uncommon.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:34.013
And to me,

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:37.283
it is sound prosecutorial discretion

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.085
They're going to have a hard

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:40.820
enough case as it is,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.487
you know, given

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:42.622
all the

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.791
the public attention around this case.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:46.793
And so I think

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:49.128
going with their strongest charges

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:49.862
against those,

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.264
you have the best proof

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:52.632
to get convictions

00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:54.767
is the right approach for prosecutors

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.135
and also charges, obviously,

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:57.537
and ultimately not brought against

00:03:57.537 --> 00:04:00.273
Lindsey Graham Perdue and Kelly Loeffler.

00:04:01.441 --> 00:04:03.376
Why not do you think

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:03.543
do you

00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:04.444
think finally Wallace was

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:06.913
was worried about being perceived

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:08.248
as being too political?

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.315
Do you think

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:12.185
it was just a matter of evidence

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:14.854
My guess is that it was a matter

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.756
of the evidence and,

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:17.924
you know, their assessment

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:18.691
of what they could prove

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.326
beyond a reasonable doubt

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.828
in the case of Graham.

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:24.030
You have some ambiguity

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.132
about what took place during that call.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.368
You don't have the definitive record

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:29.602
that we did

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:32.538
with Trump's call with Raffensperger And

00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:35.541
and so I think it's a sensible judgment,

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:38.845
the fact that the grand jurors themselves

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.079
were divided.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.648
If the vote for four grand, for

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:43.983
example, was 13 to seven,

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:46.052
that doesn't smell a lot of success

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:47.687
when you go to the higher

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:49.022
standard of proof

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:50.657
in a criminal courtroom

00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:52.792
where if you have even a single juror

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:53.893
say, no,

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:55.161
you don't get the unanimous verdict

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:56.062
that you need.

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:58.464
So I think rational judgments

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:01.401
and this case is of such great importance

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:03.136
because it goes to the heart

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:05.238
of our peaceful transfer of power.

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:06.539
We're you know

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:07.740
as members of the January six

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:08.708
committee were,

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:08.975
you know,

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.176
very proud of the work

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:12.078
we did to bring these facts

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:13.413
before the American people,

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.481
to force prosecutors to confront them

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.583
and make these difficult decisions.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:18.885
We did accountability

00:05:18.885 --> 00:05:20.019
as much as we could.

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.921
It's up to prosecutors to bring justice.

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:22.655
Yeah.

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:23.089
One other

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:23.690
note on

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:24.290
Lindsey Graham

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:24.957
is that

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.460
it's because Lindsey Graham denied

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:31.230
what what Raffensperger said.

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:33.299
He told him that Raffensperger

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:34.467
recorded the call with Trump.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:35.401
So Donald Trump

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.703
might have Lindsey Graham

00:05:36.703 --> 00:05:38.971
to thank for his Georgia indictment.

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:40.807
Congressman Adam Schiff, thanks so much.

